  <section id="lifecycle" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Engine lifecycle</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Register</strong> — the TUI/CLI calls <code>LoopEngine.upsert(def)</code> which persists the definition in <code>Storage</code> and arms (or re-arms) the interval timer in the core <code>Scheduler</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Fire</strong> — the <code>Scheduler</code> calls the engine's <code>run</code> callback. The engine claims the per-loop single-flight slot and a slot in the global <code>MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS = 3</code> pool.</li>
      <li><strong>Drive</strong> — the engine runs the stages in order. Each stage is a single <code>SessionPrompt.command(&lbrace; command: "goal" &rbrace;)</code> call. The agent's own goal loop (<code>src/session/goal.ts</code>) does the until-done iteration.</li>
      <li><strong>Persist</strong> — every stage transition renews the lease/heartbeat and writes a <code>LoopRun</code> record. Status flips to <code>complete</code>, <code>error</code>, or <code>timeout</code> when the run ends.</li>
      <li><strong>Reconcile</strong> — on next boot, <code>restore()</code> finds every <code>running</code> run whose lease is older than <code>LOOP_RUN_LEASE_MS</code> and reconciles it to <code>orphaned</code>, then re-arms every interval loop.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Single-flight guarantees">
      <p>
        The same loop can never run twice in parallel: a per-loop claim is held for the entire
        run. A second trigger that fires while the first is still active is dropped and counted
        in the run history as a fast skip.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="triggers-stages" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Triggers and stages</h2>
    <h3>Manual trigger</h3>
    <p>
      Manual loops never fire on a timer. They are started by the user through
      <code>nikcli loop run &lt;id&gt;</code>, the TUI loop dialog, or the SDK. Use a manual loop
      when the work is infrequent or you want the user to opt in every time.
    </p>
    <h3>Interval trigger</h3>
    <p>
      Interval loops fire every <code>everyMs</code> milliseconds. The smallest accepted
      interval is 30s; the largest is 30 days. The engine re-arms the timer on every run start
      and after every run finish.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="maxRuns">
      <p>
        An interval loop with <code>maxRuns</code> set will stop firing once the counter is
        reached. The loop stays in the list (paused) and you can resume it by clearing
        <code>maxRuns</code>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <h3>Stages</h3>
    <p>
      Stages run sequentially inside a single run. A stage's <code>objective</code> is the prompt
      fed to a fresh <code>goal</code> session. Stages share the loop's run id but each spawns its
      own session, so the previous stage's session is referenced by id but not reused.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text">
# A simple two-stage loop that triages issues then drafts a fix
stages:
  - name: triage
    agent: plan
    objective: "Triage the open issues in this repo and rank by impact."
  - name: draft-fix
    agent: build
    objective: "Pick the top issue and draft a fix as a draft PR."
    depends_on_run: triage
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="auto-pr" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Auto-PR on completion</h2>
    <p>
      Set <code>createPR: true</code> on a loop definition to have the engine push the worktree
      and open (or update) a GitHub pull request every time a run finishes with
      <code>status: "complete"</code>. The engine reuses one branch per loop (<code>loop/&lt;id&gt;</code>),
      so a long-running loop accumulates its work on a single PR instead of opening a fresh one
      each tick.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Best-effort">
      <p>
        The hook is intentionally best-effort: a missing <code>git</code> binary, a missing
        <code>gh</code> CLI, an unauthenticated <code>gh</code>, a non-GitHub remote, or an empty
        working tree all log a warning and let the run count as <code>complete</code> without a
        <code>pullRequest</code> on the run record.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      When the hook succeeds, the run record is updated with a <code>pullRequest</code> block
      (number, url, branch, base, action: <code>created</code> or <code>updated</code>) and the
      TUI/mobile history view deep-links straight to the PR.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cli" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>CLI subcommands</h2>
    <p>
      The CLI surface is part of the same headless engine and is the recommended way to manage
      loops from a script or cron.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Subcommand</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop list</code></td><td>List all loops for the current project.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop create</code></td><td>Create a loop interactively or with flags.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop get &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Show full definition, runtime status, and the last few runs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop run &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Trigger an immediate run (manual or interval loops).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop pause &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Stop firing triggers. In-flight runs are not aborted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop resume &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Re-enable the loop's triggers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop cancel &lt;run-id&gt;</code></td><td>Cancel an in-flight run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nikcli loop delete &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Delete the loop and its run history. Prompts for confirmation unless <code>--yes</code> is passed.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli loop create --name "triage-and-fix" \
  --stage "triage:plan:Triage open issues and rank by impact" \
  --stage "draft-fix:build:Open a draft PR for the top issue" \
  --every 30m

nikcli loop run lop_abc123
nikcli loop pause lop_abc123
nikcli loop resume lop_abc123
nikcli loop delete lop_abc123 --yes
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="bus-events" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Bus events</h2>
    <p>
      The engine publishes typed bus events for clients to subscribe to. They live next to the
      engine in <code>packages/nikcli/src/loop/engine.ts</code>.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Payload</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>loop.upserted</code></td><td><code>&#123; loopID &#125;</code></td><td>Definition created or replaced.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop.removed</code></td><td><code>&#123; loopID &#125;</code></td><td>Definition deleted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop.run.started</code></td><td><code>&#123; loopID, runID, sessionID &#125;</code></td><td>A new run started.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop.run.finished</code></td><td><code>&#123; loopID, runID, status, ok, error? &#125;</code></td><td>Run ended.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop.runtime.changed</code></td><td><code>&#123; loopID, status, lastRunAt?, lastError? &#125;</code></td><td>Live runtime status flipped (idle, running, paused, error, cancelling).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="tui" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>TUI surface</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI loop feature-plugin lives in
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/feature-plugins/loops/</code>. It mirrors the mission
      plugin and exposes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Loop home view</strong> — list of all loops with status chips, last run time, and quick actions (run, pause, resume, delete).</li>
      <li><strong>Loop detail view</strong> — per-stage list, current run highlight, run history (last 50), and the live session id of the active stage.</li>
      <li><strong>Draft dialog</strong> — pick a template, edit stages, and create a loop without leaving the TUI.</li>
      <li><strong>Run dialog</strong> — trigger an immediate run with optional one-off context (<code>--text</code>).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="mobile" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Mobile surface</h2>
    <p>
      The mobile app exposes the same surface as a dedicated <strong>Loops</strong> tab. The
      screen lists every loop with a status chip, schedule summary, last-run timestamp, and
      quick actions. The detail screen edits the definition, fires manual runs, and pages
      through run history.
    </p>
    <p>See <a href="/docs/mobile">Mobile</a> for the full mobile walkthrough.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Run stuck in <code>running</code> after a crash</td><td>The orchestrator process died; the lease has expired.</td><td>Restart the host. <code>restore()</code> reconciles the run to <code>orphaned</code> and you can trigger a fresh run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trigger never fires</td><td>The loop is <code>paused</code> or <code>disabled</code>, or <code>maxRuns</code> has been reached.</td><td>Inspect <code>nikcli loop get &lt;id&gt;</code>; resume or re-create.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stage never starts</td><td>Global cap of <code>MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS = 3</code> is saturated.</td><td>Wait for in-flight runs to finish, or cancel one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Single-flight slot held by an aborted run</td><td>Earlier process crashed without releasing the slot.</td><td>The lease reaper releases the slot after <code>LOOP_RUN_LEASE_MS</code> (15s). If it persists, restart the host.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stage stays in <code>running</code> forever</td><td>Stage worker exceeded <code>timeoutMs</code>.</td><td>The engine aborts the worker via <code>prompt.cancel</code> and marks the run <code>timeout</code>. Inspect the worker session and re-run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>createPR</code> is set but the run finishes with no <code>pullRequest</code> field</td><td>Missing git/gh, unauthenticated <code>gh</code>, non-GitHub remote, or no working-tree diff.</td><td>Check the engine logs for the warning; the run still counts as <code>complete</code>. Install/authenticate <code>gh</code>, point <code>origin</code> at a GitHub repo, or commit changes before the run ends.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

